e":"v"},{"type":"text","text":" a potential h(v), and Dijkstra’s algorithm runs from every vertex over the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"reduced weights h(u) + w - h(v), which are nonnegative. Parallel edges count by their"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"cheapest. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"findNegativeCycle(weight:)"},{"type":"text","text":" gives a negative cycle when this is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"nil"},{"type":"text","text":". On sparse"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"graphs this is faster than "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"floydWarshallShortestPaths(weight:)"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"name":"Complexity","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"O(nm) for Bellman–Ford in the worst case (about one pass when no weight is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"negative), then O(n · (n + m) log n); O(n²) space."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"style":"note","type":"aside"},{"name":"Precondition","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"No weight is NaN; every sum fits in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"W"},{"type":"text","text":", the reduced weights and the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"reduced distances included. With floating point, distances are converted back from the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"reduced ones and can differ from Floyd–Warshall’s by rounding."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"style":"note","type":"aside"}]}],"kind":"symbol","schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"major":0,"minor":3},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Shortest paths between every ordered pair of vertices by Johnson’s algorithm, or "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"nil"},{"type":"text","text":" when"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the graph has a negative cycle anywhere (a negative loop counts)."}],"sections":[],"references":{"doc://ShortestPaths/documentation/ShortestPaths/GraphProtocols/DirectedGraph":{"identifier":